From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 00:05:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] library: an apply over a library that has lost its text
 leaves the agents' parts alone
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A sync — the write every start does over the whole library, and the one
every change does beside it — that finds the library's text gone and an
agent's file still holding magpie's part wrote the empty state over it:
nine agents' files wiped to match a Library an upgrade had emptied, four
of them deleted whole, nothing asked (#5709, lennonli; the parts out
there were the only copy of the instructions left, and only magpie's own
backups brought them back). The library's record of what it last wrote
an agent read as the hash of empty — it knew, and wrote it anyway.

Three things now stand between an apply and a wipe:

- A sync that would write nothing over a part, while the library's text
  itself is gone, writes nothing and says so instead, one line an agent:
  the part comes back with the library's next text, and the record of
  the last write reading as the hash of empty — a library reset under
  the agents — is named in the line. A text the library still holds
  with one agent unticked takes back as usual, and the page's own
  take-back (a write made with the library as the user left it) is
  untouched: switching an agent off, a set cleared, a distro's part
  taken out once it runs, all as they were.
- An apply never takes a whole file away: what it empties it empties in
  place, the file staying for the agent that reads it.
- A part whose agent the library can give nothing to, with nothing kept
  of the text, is the red flag the reporter read out of library.json
  (instrHash the hash of the empty string); it is logged when the apply
  passes a file it leaves alone.

The wipe was reached at the Remove of syncInstructions, where a file
that held nothing of the user's went whole; that path stays for the
page's writes and is closed to an apply.

